;; A line-sequential file of the kind an adopter is likeliest to hand the tool
;; first: ASCII, on Linux, its records ended by 0x0A.
;;
;; Charset governs items and never framing bytes, so an ASCII twin of
;; delimited-terminator would say only what packed-ascii and packed-ebcdic
;; already say about an axis that does not apply, and would not be worth an
;; entry. What earns this one is the collision. A PIC S9(4) COMP item holding 10
;; is the bytes 00 0A and one holding 2570 is 0A 0A, and 0x0A is what ends a
;; record here — so a consumer that searched the input for the delimiter cuts
;; the first record after five bytes and the second after four and again after
;; five, while one that counts the extent reads them as the numbers they are.
;; That is delimited-terminator's point in the file the field actually holds.
;;
;; The third record's count is zero, so its six bytes carry no 0x0A at all: it
;; is the ordinary record the other two are read against, and it is what a
;; consumer that cut the file into four records or five would still have got
;; right.
;;
;; Nothing in Appendix A describes a delimiter — codec/SPEC.md places record
;; formats out of scope in as many words — so this entry's framing is derived
;; from the two specifications this repository publishes rather than from
;; cobol-go's, exactly as delimited-terminator's is.
(encoding
  (charset ascii)
  (sign-convention ascii-zone-37)
  (byte-order big-endian)
  (float-format ieee-754))

(framing
  (recfm line-sequential)
  (delimiter (bytes "0A"))
  (placement terminator))

(record LINE
  (copybook "line.cpy" LINE-RECORD))

(discriminate LINE single-record-type)

(sequence (* LINE))
